This project has multiple sub-parts, each sub-part is intended to be run from its respective directory, due to python imports
Sub-Part
    Trivial Game:
        BackGammon as simple as possible, 
            Double Moves:               No
            Chance:                     No
            Board Size:                 6
            Die Size/Max Step Size:     2
            Home Size == Die Size:      2
            Number of Pieces:           2

    Reduced Game:
        BackGammon as simple as possible, Scaled Up, 
            Double Moves:               No
            Chance:                     No
            Board Size:                 Variable
            Die Size/Max Step Size:     Variable
            Home Size == Die Size:      Variable
            Number of Pieces:           Variable

    ChanceReducedGame:
        BackGammon only Missing Double Moves, 
            Double Moves:               No
            Chance:                     Yes
            Board Size:                 Variable
            Die Size/Max Step Size:     Variable
            Home Size == Die Size:      Variable
            Number of Pieces:           Variable

To change the Board size, Die Size, Home Size, Number of Pieces, there are Constants in Board.py that can be changed.
However, if you change the Number of Pieces, you will need to edit the Board __init__ to have that number of pieces 

All Games can be run by running
    python ./Backgammon.py -p1 "agent.MCTSAgent MCTSAgent" -p2 "agent.A0Agent A0Agent"
or more generaly
    python ./Backgammon.py -p1 "filepath object" -p2 "filepath object"

-t to turn timelimit off
-v to use verbose mode

sub-parts train_model and parallel are made to train on the UoM CSF3 / SLURM.
while the A0Trainer.py file in each sub-part will train an A0Agent on any board eventualy, it can be very slow and timescales get very large, very fast, scaling with gamelength, branching factor and number of simulations.
Additionaly, the SLURM training scripts also include random starting positions, which improve robustness of the network